// TASK 2: Why Threat Model
$task_contents[2] = <<<'HTML'
<div class="content-block">
    <h2>Finding Problems on Paper, Not in Production</h2>

    <p>Threat modelling is a structured way of asking three questions about a system before it goes live: what are we building, what can go wrong with it, and what are we going to do about it. Answering these questions early turns security into a design decision instead of an emergency response.</p>

    <p>Think of it the way a structural engineer reviews a building blueprint before construction starts. A flaw caught on paper costs a redrawn wall. The same flaw caught after the building is occupied costs a demolition and a lawsuit. Software works the same way, except the "walls" are authentication checks, network boundaries, and data flows.</p>

    <div class="info-box">
        <h4><i class="fas fa-info-circle"></i> Why This Matters</h4>
        <p>Threat modelling is a repeatable process built into design reviews, not a one-time audit performed once and forgotten. Every significant change to a system is an opportunity to threat model it again.</p>
    </div>

