// TASK 5: Attack Trees
$task_contents[5] = <<<'HTML'
<div class="content-block">
    <h2>Mapping the Attacker's Path</h2>

    <p>An attack tree starts with a single high-level goal at the root, such as "compromise customer data," and breaks it down into the concrete steps an attacker could take to reach it. Each branch represents one possible path.</p>

    <p>Branches use two kinds of logic. An OR branch means any one of the child steps is enough on its own. An AND branch means every child step must succeed together before the parent goal is reached. This distinction is what lets you identify the attacker's cheapest path, since a path made entirely of easy OR steps is usually more dangerous than one requiring several difficult AND steps.</p>

    <!-- SVG: simple attack tree with OR and AND branches
         Teaching purpose: show the learner that AND/OR logic determines the actual
         easiest path through the tree, which they would otherwise read as a generic
         flowchart with no logical weighting between branches. -->
    <div style="margin: 20px 0;">
        <svg viewBox="0 0 700 280" width="100%" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg" role="img" aria-label="Attack tree with OR and AND branches">
            <rect x="270" y="10" width="160" height="50" rx="8" fill="var(--surface)" stroke="var(--primary)" stroke-width="1.5"/>
            <text x="350" y="40" text-anchor="middle" font-size="12" font-weight="600" fill="var(--text-primary)">Compromise Data</text>

            <line x1="350" y1="60" x2="180" y2="110" stroke="var(--text-secondary)" stroke-width="1.5"/>
            <line x1="350" y1="60" x2="520" y2="110" stroke="var(--text-secondary)" stroke-width="1.5"/>
            <text x="350" y="85" text-anchor="middle" font-size="11" fill="var(--text-secondary)">OR</text>

            <rect x="100" y="110" width="160" height="50" rx="8" fill="var(--surface)" stroke="var(--border)" stroke-width="1.5"/>
            <text x="180" y="140" text-anchor="middle" font-size="12" fill="var(--text-primary)">Compromise Endpoint</text>

