// TASK 6: Trust Boundaries and the Threat Register
$task_contents[6] = <<<'HTML'
<div class="content-block">
    <h2>Where Trust Changes, Controls Belong</h2>

    <p>A trust boundary is any point where the level of trust changes between two components, such as where the public internet meets an internal network, or where an internal service meets a third-party integration. Controls like authentication and input validation belong exactly at these boundaries.</p>

    <p>Decomposing a system, applying STRIDE, and scoring risk all feed into a single deliverable called a threat register. Each row records the asset, the threat, its STRIDE category, an impact and likelihood score, the resulting risk score, and a priority. This register is what security teams actually use to decide which controls to build first.</p>

    <table class="content-table">
        <thead>
            <tr>
                <th>Asset</th>
                <th>Threat</th>
                <th>Category</th>
                <th>Risk Score</th>
            </tr>
        </thead>
        <tbody>
            <tr><td>API Gateway</td><td>Request flooding causes downtime</td><td>Denial of Service</td><td>20</td></tr>
            <tr><td>Records Database</td><td>Privilege escalation exposes records</td><td>Elevation of Privilege</td><td>16</td></tr>
            <tr><td>Admin Console</td><td>Unlogged record modification</td><td>Repudiation</td><td>15</td></tr>
        </tbody>
    </table>

    <div class="success-box">
        <h4><i class="fas fa-lightbulb"></i> Key Point</h4>
        <p>A threat register is what drives control selection. The modelling exercise itself is only useful once it produces a prioritised list someone can act on.</p>
    </div>
</div>
HTML;

